            <section class="mt-content-container">
                
          <p>The Hamiltonian for this displaced harmonic oscillator problem bears a notable similarity to a general form for the interaction of an electronic &ldquo;system&rdquo; that is directly observed in an experiment with a harmonic oscillator &ldquo;bath&rdquo; whose degrees of freedom are not directly observed but influence the behavior of the system. This reasoning will in fact be developed more carefully later for the description of fluctuations. Coupling to a continuum, or a harmonic bath, is the starting point for developing how an electronic system interacts with a continuum of intermolecular motions and phonons typical of condensed phase systems. While the Hamiltonians we have written so far describe coupling to a single bath degree of freedom, the DHO model is readily generalized to many vibrations or a continuum of nuclear motions.</p>

<p>So, what happens if the electronic transition is coupled to many vibrational coordinates, each with its own displacement? The extension is straightforward if we continue to consider only two electronic states (<span class="math inline">\(e\)</span> and <span class="math inline">\(g\)</span>) to which we couple a set of independent modes, i.e., a bath of harmonic normal modes that interact with the system but not with each other. Then, using eq. (<a data-reference="17.1.5" data-reference-type="ref" title="Workbench/TDQMS/17:_Coupling_of_Electronic_and_Nuclear_Motion/16.01:_The_Displaced_Harmonic_Oscillator_Model#mjx-eqn-eq17.1.05" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/Time-Dependent_Quantum_Mechanics_and_Spectroscopy_2025e_(Tokmakoff)/16%3A_Coupling_of_Electronic_and_Nuclear_Motion/16.01%3A_The_Displaced_Harmonic_Oscillator_Model#mjx-eqn-eq17.1.05" rel="internal">17.1.5</a>), we can write the Hamiltonian for <span class="math inline">\(N\)</span> vibrations as a sum over all the independent harmonic modes, indexed by <span class="math inline">\(\alpha\)</span></p>

<p><span class="math display">\[H_e = \sum\limits_{\alpha = 1}^{N} H_e^{(\alpha )} = \sum\limits_{\alpha = 1}^{N} \left( \frac{p_\alpha ^{2}}{2m_{\alpha }} + \frac{1}{2}m_{\alpha }\omega _\alpha ^{2} \left( q_{\alpha } - d_{\alpha } \right)^{2} \right) \label{eq17.2.01}\]</span></p>

<p>Each normal mode is written with their own distinct frequency and displacement. We can specify the state of the system in terms of product states in the electronic and nuclear occupation, i.e.,</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[\begin{array}{rl} \left| G \right\rangle \kern-.8em &amp;= \left| g;n_{1},n_{2},...,n_{N} \right\rangle \\ &amp;= \left| g \right\rangle \displaystyle \prod\limits_{\alpha = 1}^{N} \left| n_{\alpha } \right\rangle \end{array} \label{eq17.2.02}\]</span></p>

<p>Additionally, we recognize that the time propagator on the electronically excited potential energy surface is a product of propagators on the potential for each normal mode coordinate.</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[U_e = \exp \left[ - \frac{i}{\hbar }H_{e}t \right] = \prod\limits_{\alpha = 1}^{N} U_e^{(\alpha )} \label{eq17.2.03}\]</span> where<span class="math display">\[U_e^{(\alpha )} = \exp \left[ - \frac{i}{\hbar }H_e^{(\alpha )}t \right] \label{eq17.2.04}\]</span></p>

<p>Similarly, for each normal mode, there is a unique Huang-Rhys factor</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[S_{\alpha } = d_\alpha ^{2}({ m\omega _{\alpha } / 2\hbar }) \label{eq17.2.05}\]</span></p>

<p>dephasing function</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[F_{}^{(\alpha )} = \left\langle \left[ U_g^{(\alpha )} \right]^{\dagger }U_e^{(\alpha )} \right\rangle \label{eq17.2.06}\]</span></p>

<p>and lineshape function <span class="math inline">\(g_\alpha=-\ln{F^\alpha}.\)</span> The dipole correlation function is then just a product of multiple dephasing functions that characterize the time-evolution of the different vibrations.</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[C_{\mu \mu }\left( t \right) = \left| \mu _{eg} \right|^{2} e^{ - i\omega _{eg}t} \cdot \prod\limits_{\alpha = 1}^{N} F_{}^{(\alpha )}\left( t \right) \label{eq17.2.07}\]</span></p>

<p>or equivalently a sum of different lineshape functions:</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[C_{\mu \mu }\left( t \right) = \left| \mu _{eg} \right|^{2} e^{ - i\omega _{eg}t - g\left( t \right)} \label{eq17.2.08}\]</span></p>

<p>with<span class="math display">\[g(t) = - \sum\limits_{\alpha } g_{\alpha }(t) \label{eq17.2.09}\]</span></p>

<p>Taking the case that the system is initially in the ground electronic state with all normal mode vibrations in <span class="math inline">\(n_\alpha=0\)</span>, the initial state of the system can be written <span class="math inline">\(\left|g, \mathbf{0}&nbsp;\right\rangle\)</span>, and the lineshape function is</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[g\left( t \right) = - \sum\limits_{\alpha } S_{\alpha }\left( e^{ - i\omega _{\alpha }t} - 1 \right) \label{eq17.2.10}\]</span></p>

<p>In the time domain this is a complex beating pattern. In the frequency domain, this appears as a spectrum with several superimposed vibronic progressions that follow the rules developed above.</p>

<p>Taking this a step further, the generalization to a continuum of nuclear states emerges naturally in the frequency domain. We define a harmonic density of states, <span class="math inline">\(W\left(\omega\right)\)</span>, that characterizes the continuous frequency distribution of normal modes, and generalize eq. (\ref{eq17.2.05})&nbsp;to a continuously varying coupling of the electronic system to the modes,&nbsp;<span class="math inline">\(S\left(\omega\right)\)</span>, allowing us to change the sum in eq. (\ref{eq17.2.09}) to an integral over the density of states. For instance,</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[g\left( t \right) = \int_{0}^{\infty } d\omega {\kern 1pt} W\left( \omega \right) S\left( \omega \right) \left( e^{ - i\omega t} - 1 \right) \label{eq17.2.11}\]</span></p>

<p>Here the product <span class="math inline">\(W(\omega ){\kern 1pt} S(\omega )\)</span> is a coupling-weighted density of states and is commonly referred to as a <em>spectral density</em>.</p>

<p>This treatment establishes a bath of states with which the experimentally observed electronic transition may interact. Coupling to a bath or continuum introduces relaxation effects or damping of the electronic coherence in the absorption spectrum. You can see that if <span class="math inline">\(g\left( t \right)\)</span> is associated with a real constant <span class="math inline">\(\mathrm{\Gamma}\)</span> that reflects an irreversible relaxation process, we obtain a Lorentzian lineshape with width <span class="math inline">\(\mathrm{\Gamma}\)</span>. This lineshape behavior emerges under certain circumstances, such as the distribution of states and coupling being large and constant, or the integration of eq. (\ref{eq17.2.11}) over a distribution of low frequencies, such that <span class="math inline">\(e^{ - i\omega t} \approx 1 - i\omega t\)</span>. More generally the real part of the lineshape describes damping and the imaginary part shifts and modulates the primary frequency and leads to frequency shifts and fine structure.</p>

<span id="An_Ensemble_at_Finite_Temperature"></span><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/Time-Dependent_Quantum_Mechanics_and_Spectroscopy_2025e_(Tokmakoff)/16:_Coupling_of_Electronic_and_Nuclear_Motion/16.02:_Coupling_to_a_Harmonic_Bath" class="mt-section" id="section_1"><span id="An_Ensemble_at_Finite_Temperature"></span><h3 class="editable">An Ensemble at Finite Temperature</h3>

<p>The single mode DHO model described above is for a pure state, but the approach can be readily extended to describe a canonical ensemble of displaced harmonic oscillators. Again, we assume that the molecular response is governed by the single mode DHO Hamiltonian. However, for a molecular ensemble, the correlation function is averaged over a thermal distribution of the initial state of the system. Taking the initial state of the system to be in the electronic ground state and that the vibrational levels (<span class="math inline">\(n_g\)</span>) are populated following a Boltzmann distribution, the thermal distribution is:</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[p(n_{g}) = \frac{e^{ - \beta n_{g}\hbar \omega _{0}}}{Z} \label{eq17.2.12}\]</span></p>

<p>We can use this distribution to write the dipole correlation function with a thermally-averaged dephasing function:</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[C_{\mu \mu }\left( t \right) = \left| \mu _{eg} \right|^{2} e^{ - i\omega _{eg}t} F\left( t \right) \label{eq17.2.13}\]</span></p>

<p><span class="math display">\[F(t) = \sum\limits_{n_{g}} p(n_{g})\left\langle n_{g} \right| U_g^{\dagger }U_{e}\left| n_{g} \right\rangle \label{eq17.2.14}\]</span></p>

<p>Evaluating these expressions using the strategies developed earlier in this chapter leads to</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[C_{\mu \mu }\left( t \right) = \left| \mu _{eg} \right|^{2} e^{ - i\omega _{eg}t} \exp \left[ S\left[ \left( \bar n + 1 \right)\left( e^{ - i\omega _{0}t} - 1 \right) + \bar n\left( e^{i\omega _{0}t} - 1 \right) \right] \right] \label{eq17.2.15}\]</span></p>

<p>where <span class="math inline">\(\bar n\)</span> is the thermally averaged occupation number of the harmonic vibrational mode in the ground state.</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[\bar n = \left( e^{\beta \hbar \omega _{0}} - 1 \right)^{ - 1} \label{eq17.2.16}\]</span></p>

<p>The dephasing function in (\ref{eq17.2.15}) has two terms which each correlate to different changes in the vibrational quantum number. The first is proportional to <span class="math inline">\(\left( \bar n + 1 \right)\)</span> and describes those electronic absorption events in which the vibrational quantum number increases or is unchanged (<span class="math inline">\(n_e\geq\ n_g\)</span>). The second one is proportional to <span class="math inline">\(\bar n\)</span>, describing those processes where the vibrational quantum number decreases or is unchanged (<span class="math inline">\(n_e\le\ n_g\)</span>). The latter are only allowed at elevated temperatures, where thermally excited states are populated and are known as &ldquo;hot bands.&rdquo; In the low temperature limit, <span class="math inline">\(\bar n \to 0\)</span>, and eq. (\ref{eq17.2.15}) equals our original result, eq. (<a title="Workbench/TDQMS/17:_Coupling_of_Electronic_and_Nuclear_Motion/16.01:_The_Displaced_Harmonic_Oscillator_Model#mjx-eqn-eq17.1.31" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/Time-Dependent_Quantum_Mechanics_and_Spectroscopy_2025e_(Tokmakoff)/16%3A_Coupling_of_Electronic_and_Nuclear_Motion/16.01%3A_The_Displaced_Harmonic_Oscillator_Model#mjx-eqn-eq17.1.31" rel="internal">17.1.31</a>).</p>

<p>Now we calculate the lineshape corresponding to the correlation function in eq. (\ref{eq17.2.15}). If we separate the dephasing function into a product of two exponential terms and expand each of these exponentials, we can Fourier transform the result to find</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[ \sigma _{abs}\left( \omega \right) = \left| \mu _{eg} \right|^{2} e^{ - S\left( 2\bar n + 1 \right)}\sum\limits_{j = 0}^{\infty } \sum\limits_{k = 0}^{\infty } \left( \frac{S^{j + k}}{j!k!} \right) \left( \bar n + 1 \right)^{j}{\bar n}^{k} \delta \left( \omega - \omega _{eg} - \left( j - k \right)\omega _{0} \right) \label{eq17.2.17}\]</span></p>

<p>Here, the summation over <span class="math inline">\(j\)</span> describes <span class="math inline">\(n_e\geq\ n_g\)</span> transitions, whereas the summation over <span class="math inline">\(k\)</span> describes <span class="math inline">\(n_e\le\ n_g\)</span>. For any one transition frequency, <span class="math inline">\((\omega_{eg}+n\omega_0)\)</span>, the net absorption is a sum over all possible combination of transitions within the energy splitting, given <span class="math inline">\(n=(j-k)\)</span>. Again, if we set <span class="math inline">\(\bar n \to 0\)</span>, we obtain our original result eq. (<a data-reference="17.1.36" data-reference-type="ref" title="Workbench/TDQMS/17:_Coupling_of_Electronic_and_Nuclear_Motion/16.01:_The_Displaced_Harmonic_Oscillator_Model#mjx-eqn-eq17.1.36" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/Time-Dependent_Quantum_Mechanics_and_Spectroscopy_2025e_(Tokmakoff)/16%3A_Coupling_of_Electronic_and_Nuclear_Motion/16.01%3A_The_Displaced_Harmonic_Oscillator_Model#mjx-eqn-eq17.1.36" rel="internal">17.1.36</a>). The contributions where <span class="math inline">\(k&lt;j\)</span> lead to the hot bands.</p>

<div class="row" role="group">
