</figure>
</div>

<p>Combining these results with those of the previous section, we can describe the influence of temperature on the coupling of an electronic transition to many independent normal modes or to a continuum. To achieve the former, we once again describe the system as a product state in the vibrational quantum numbers, i.e., <span class="math inline">\(\left| E \right\rangle = \left| e;n_{1},n_{2},n_{3} \ldots \right\rangle\)</span>. The resulting lineshape function is</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[g(t) = - \sum\limits_{\alpha } S_{\alpha }\left[ \left( {\bar n}_{\alpha } + 1 \right)\left( e^{ - i\omega _{\alpha }t} - 1 \right) + {\bar n}_{\alpha }\left( e^{i\omega _{\alpha }t} - 1 \right) \right] \label{eq17.2.18}\]</span></p>

<p>Alternatively, we can describe coupling to a continuum by changing to an integral over a continuous frequency distribution of normal modes. This distribution is characterized by a density of states, <span class="math inline">\(W\left( \omega \right)\)</span>, and frequency-dependent coupling, <span class="math inline">\(S\left( \omega \right)\)</span>, leading to the lineshape</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[g\left( t \right) = - \int_{0}^{\infty } d\omega {\kern 1pt} W\left( \omega \right) S\left( \omega \right) \left[ \left( \bar n\left( \omega \right) + 1 \right)\left( e^{ - i\omega t} - 1 \right) + \bar n\left( \omega \right)\left( e^{i\omega t} - 1 \right) \right] \label{eq17.2.19}\]</span></p>

<p>Similar to our earlier results, the limit <span class="math inline">\(\bar{n}\rightarrow0\)</span> occurs at temperature <span class="math inline">\(T=0\)</span>.</p>

<p>Let&rsquo;s look at the envelope of the electronic absorption lineshape that emerges from coupling to a continuum. We begin by performing a short-time expansion on the exponential as in eq. (<a data-reference="17.1.38" data-reference-type="ref" title="Workbench/TDQMS/17:_Coupling_of_Electronic_and_Nuclear_Motion/16.01:_The_Displaced_Harmonic_Oscillator_Model#mjx-eqn-eq17.1.39" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/Time-Dependent_Quantum_Mechanics_and_Spectroscopy_2025e_(Tokmakoff)/16%3A_Coupling_of_Electronic_and_Nuclear_Motion/16.01%3A_The_Displaced_Harmonic_Oscillator_Model#mjx-eqn-eq17.1.39" rel="internal">17.1.39</a>)</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[F\left( t \right) = e^{ - g(t)} = \exp \left[ \int_{0}^{\infty } d\omega {\kern 1pt} W\left( \omega \right) S\left( \omega \right)\left( - i\omega t - \left( 2\bar n + 1 \right)\frac{\omega ^{2}t^{2}}{2} \right) \right] \label{eq17.2.20}\]</span></p>

<p>The absorption lineshape is calculated from</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[\sigma _{abs}\left( \omega \right) = \int_{ - \infty }^{ + \infty } dt \, e^{i(\omega - \omega _{eg})t} \exp \left[ - i \left\langle \omega \right\rangle t \right] \exp \left[ - \frac{1}{2}\left\langle \omega ^{2} \right\rangle t^{2} \right] \label{eq17.2.21}\]</span></p>

<p>where we have defined the mean vibrational excitation upon absorption as</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[\begin{array}{rl} \left\langle \omega \right\rangle&nbsp;\kern-0.8em&nbsp;&amp;= \displaystyle\int_{0}^{\infty } d\omega \, \omega W\left( \omega \right) S\left( \omega \right) \\ &amp;= \lambda /\hbar \end{array} \label{eq17.2.22}\]</span></p>

<p>and</p>

<p><span class="math display">\[\left\langle \omega ^{2} \right\rangle = \int_{0}^{\infty } d\omega \, \omega ^{2}{\kern 1pt} W\left( \omega \right) S\left( \omega \right)\left( 2\bar n(\omega ) + 1 \right) \label{eq17.2.23}\]</span></p>

<p><span class="math display">\(\left\langle \omega ^{2} \right\rangle\)&nbsp;reflects the thermally averaged distribution of accessible vibrational states. After completing the square, eq. (\ref{eq17.2.21}) gives </span></p>

<p><span class="math display">\[\sigma _{abs}\left( \omega \right) = \left| \mu _{eg} \right|^{2} \sqrt {\frac{2\pi }{\left\langle \omega ^{2} \right\rangle } } \exp \left[ \frac{ - \left( \omega - \omega _{eg} - \left\langle \omega \right\rangle \right)^{2}}{2\left\langle \omega ^{2} \right\rangle } \right] \label{eq17.2.24}\]</span></p>

<p>The lineshape is Gaussian, with a peak frequency at <span class="math inline">\(\omega_{eg}+\langle\omega\rangle\)</span> and a Gaussian width governed by <span class="math inline">\(\left\langle \omega ^{2} \right\rangle\)</span>. The width depends on the strength of coupling to the bath and increases with the degree of temperature-dependence, due to the thermal occupation factor in eq. (\ref{eq17.2.23}).</p>

